<h2>The case in one view</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Case</th><td>DDA v. Jaksons Developers (P) Ltd., FAO(OS) (COMM) 192/2026</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Bench</th><td>Dinesh Mehta and Dr. Aditi Choudhary, JJ., High Court of Delhi</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Pronounced</th><td>6 November 2026 (reserved 13 October 2026)</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Provisions</th><td>Sections 17, 31(7), 33, 34 and 37, Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Outcome</th><td>Appeal dismissed; developer entitled to the FDR interest</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The background</h2>
<p>DDA allotted the developer a hotel plot in Rohini in January 2008 for the 2010 Commonwealth Games, secured by a Rs 9.30 crore performance bank guarantee. Alleging delay, DDA moved to invoke the guarantee in May 2010; stays under Sections 9 and 17 intervened; the hotel became operational in September 2010 and was used for the Games. In October 2013 the arbitrator permitted DDA to encash the guarantee, but on terms: the proceeds would sit in a fixed deposit, and the interest would abide the outcome of the arbitration.</p>
<p>The 2023 award went against DDA on the central question — it was never entitled to invoke the guarantee — and ordered the Rs 9.30 crore released to the developer. But by an addendum under Section 33, the arbitrator withheld the FDR interest, reasoning that the developer&rsquo;s separate interest claim had been rejected. The developer&rsquo;s limited Section 34 challenge to that withholding succeeded before a Single Judge in May 2026. DDA appealed.</p>

<h2>Internal inconsistency as patent illegality</h2>
<p>The Division Bench&rsquo;s reasoning runs in a straight line. The FDR was a creature of the Section 17 interim order — a holding arrangement whose whole point was that the money&rsquo;s ultimate owner would take it with its earnings. Once the award held DDA&rsquo;s invocation wrongful, allowing DDA to keep the interest inverted that arrangement and contradicted the award&rsquo;s own logic, the more so as DDA&rsquo;s counter-claim for interest on the guarantee had itself been rejected.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> &ldquo;There is no reasoned finding recorded in the award for denying interest accrued on the FDR to JDPL.&rdquo; And the principle, in a phrase: &ldquo;the interest of the FDR is nothing but the fruit of the principal amount of the FDR.&rdquo;</p>
</div>
<p>Section 31(7) did not assist DDA, because the encashed guarantee was not &ldquo;the sum for which the award is made&rdquo;; the FDR interest was not award interest at all, but an accretion to secured money.</p>

<h2>The Gayatri Balasamy objection</h2>
<p>DDA&rsquo;s structural argument was that the Section 34 court had modified the award, which courts cannot do. The Bench disagreed on severability: the developer&rsquo;s rejected interest claim (Claim 8) never encompassed the FDR interest, so the withholding direction stood apart from the merits and could be severed without rewriting the award. The distinction matters after <em>Gayatri Balasamy</em>: setting aside a severable, inconsistent direction is within Section 34; substituting the court&rsquo;s view on a decided claim is not.</p>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For parties to interim security arrangements.</strong> When encashed guarantees or deposits are parked in FDRs &ldquo;to abide the outcome&rdquo;, the interest follows the principal. Draft interim orders, and later submissions, on that footing.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For award-challengers.</strong> A surgical Section 34 challenge aimed at one internally inconsistent direction — rather than the whole award — succeeded here and survived appeal. Severability is a strategy, not just a doctrine.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For public authorities.</strong> An invocation ultimately held wrongful carries restitutionary consequences in full: principal, and its fruits. Holding the money through years of litigation earns nothing.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>The judgment sits at the intersection of two live themes in Indian arbitration law: the limits on judicial interference with awards after <em>Gayatri Balasamy</em>, and the restitutionary logic governing security furnished under interim orders. The Division Bench&rsquo;s resolution — interference confined to a severable inconsistency, restitution extended to the fruits of the secured sum — offers a tidy template for both. For infrastructure and real-estate disputes in Delhi, where performance guarantees are invoked early and litigated for a decade, the practical rule is now crisply stated at Division Bench level.</p>
